Z||000000||maryjane||05-01-2001||01:53 AM||||My 84 4cyl(air,auto trans),150,000 miles,is due oil& filter change. It is already getting hot out here in West Texas so I am thinking of going to a higher vis oil.It is a daily driver and gives no trouble in town but on long trips the valves seem to clatter a little. What oil would be best for this car? You all have been a wealth of information and sometimes amusement(it's always funny when it is happening to the other guy) Thanks a lot||||reg||9
Z||000001||Phaeton||05-01-2001||02:44 AM||||These synthetics are so good I'm considering using 0w-30 year round.<P>But on an older engine something thicker to fill in the gaps may be nice, say a 5w-50 synthetic.<P>I have heard reliable rumors that conventional oil is a little slipperier for a given viscosity, but the dirt carrying capacity of synthetics wins me over every time.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Spektyr||05-01-2001||04:21 AM||||If you put synthetic oil in a car that's been running on regular oil (especially one with 150k miles) you'd better invest in a LOT of oil and kitty litter.<P>You're going to turn your car into a machine for depositing oil on your driveway.<P>Regular oil is filled with impurities that like to stick to things, like your gaskets.  They work their way into the gaskets and bond to them.  Then the synthetic comes along with it's fancy detergents and cleans all that up, along with the gasket and bingo, instant oil leak.  (At least that's how I had it described to me.)<P>Just ask the 1981 Ford Granada I had.  Ran great, didn't burn oil, didn't leak oil.  I switch to a synthetic oil and it started leaking like a sieve.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000003||grinthock||05-01-2001||08:59 AM||||I ues 20W50 as per my mechanic, I was told to use 5w30 for the breakin period then run 20w50, some saythat the higher weight oil is bad for it, but he says you get better protection when the car is started cold, and at high RPM's when the oil is hot and runny....  espcially because I use the track, FieroShop uses 20W50 in all their personal vehicles.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||05-01-2001||07:41 PM||||Spektyr... Unless the car is already having problems then Synthetic, such as Mobil 1, is very unlikely to do what you have been told. This is a hold over rumor from very early synthetics that did not have suficient plastic conditioners in them. That problem was solved long ago.<P>grinthock... While 20W-50 works wonders on the track it is very often not a good thing for most cars.<P>The oil specified for an engine is based on how the engine is built and to what tolerances it is built. Putting heavier oil in an engine than what it was made to run can hurt it.<P>Thicker oil can also cause More cold start problems than it might seem to fix. It takes longer to charge the system to operating pressure. Anything that delays that process is hurting the engine.<P>maryjane<P>Before you change from the OE oil spec You should understand the 84 recalls and if they have been completed. (The links to this info are in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A> Your mechanic can use that info to tell if the work was done.) You also need to know if this is the original 84 motor or one that was replaced. <P>Since it's obviously too hot to worry much about the winter number, you might want to try a 10w-30 or 10w-40 synthetic or sythetic blend. (The blend is a mix of regular and synthetic.)<P>If you are having hot lifter clatter then you should keep an eye on it. This can be a sign the valve train needs work. Just changing the oil may help or it may not. depends just what is going on.||||reg||1
